{"kind":"request","text":"codex: make chess serve fail when the repository is not there, instead of starting and telling only the browser. Hugh reported this today and I have reproduced it.\n\nWHAT HUGH REPORTED. \"chess serve should exit/fail if the repository doesn't exist, currently reports only to the browser.\"\n\nWHAT I REPRODUCED, at current main a1f130f. Built cmd/chess and ran it against a path that does not exist. chess serve -repo /nonexistent/nope -listen 127.0.0.1:18471 prints http://127.0.0.1:18471 to stdout, stays running, binds the port, and answers requests with the body \"repository is unavailable\". The process never exits and never reports a failure to whoever started it. For contrast I ran a read subcommand against the same missing path: chess board -repo /nonexistent/nope -game x fails immediately and loudly with \"chess: resolve git dirs: exit status 128: fatal: cannot change to '/nonexistent/nope': No such file or directory\". So the rest of the command surface already behaves correctly and serve is the outlier.\n\nWHERE IT COMES FROM, as I read it. runServe in cmd/chess/main.go parses flags, checks only that the listen address is loopback, then constructs the server with newReadHandler and calls ListenAndServe. It never opens the repository. Every read handler calls application.OpenProjection per request, so the first time anything touches the repository is inside an HTTP request, and the only channel the failure has is the response body. Compare openWriter in the same file, which calls host.Open up front and returns the error to the caller. The fix is to give serve the same up-front check, not to change how the handlers behave.\n\nWHY THIS IS ITS OWN LANE AND NOT PART OF THE FIRST-USER HEAD. runServe is byte-identical between main a1f130f and the approved head b9483eaa — I compared them — so that head neither causes this nor fixes it, and folding a repair into it would invalidate approval cba97723 and send reviewed work back to step 3. Hugh has just released that merge, so this sits behind it and lands on the resulting main.\n\nONE THING TO PRESERVE. The browser response is not wrong in itself; a repository can also disappear while the server is running, and that case should still answer rather than crash. What is wrong is that it is the only channel. Add the startup refusal and keep the runtime handling.","body":{"conditions":"Sequenced after the first-user head b9483eaa merges; implement on the resulting main, not by amending that approved head. chess serve validates the repository before it listens: when the repository is absent, unopenable, or not a chess workroom, the command returns an error and exits non-zero without printing a listen URL and without binding the port. The existing per-request handling stays: a repository that becomes unavailable after a successful start must still produce the current browser response rather than crashing the process, so the startup check is an addition and not a replacement. At least one regression test drives the wired command with a nonexistent -repo and asserts a non-zero exit, no URL on stdout, and no listener bound; the assertion must be written from the desired behaviour and must fail against current main. Error text names the repository path and the reason, in the manner of the existing read subcommands. gofmt, go build, go vet and the full suite green at the exact head with -count=1; exact clean pushed head with per-path artifacts; independent review before merge.","severity":"normal","to":"09c97907402e7747d051c0c599d9eb463c48d19825820a3e4c433cde2e60e2a8"}}